### How do you implement a rate-limiter interceptor?

Keep a counter or token bucket keyed by client identity. Reject excess traffic with `RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED`.

```java
package com.bank.grpc;

import com.google.common.util.concurrent.RateLimiter;
import io.grpc.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;

public class RateLimiterInterceptor implements ServerInterceptor {
    // Per-client rate limiter: 100 requests/second
    private final ConcurrentMap<String, RateLimiter> limiters
            = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    @Override
    public <ReqT, RespT> ServerCall.Listener<ReqT> interceptCall(
            ServerCall<ReqT, RespT> call,
            Metadata headers,
            ServerCallHandler<ReqT, RespT> next) {

        String clientId = call.getAttributes()
                .get(Grpc.TRANSPORT_ATTR_REMOTE_ADDR).toString();

        RateLimiter limiter = limiters.computeIfAbsent(
                clientId, k -> RateLimiter.create(100.0));

        if (!limiter.tryAcquire()) {
            // Reject: too many requests
            call.close(Status.RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED
                    .withDescription("Rate limit exceeded for " + clientId),
                    new Metadata());
            return new ServerCall.Listener<>() {}; // empty listener
        }

        return next.startCall(call, headers);
    }
}
```

### How do you register interceptors on a gRPC server?

Interceptors are added to the server builder and execute in order for every incoming call.

```java
package com.bank.grpc;

import io.grpc.Server;
import io.grpc.ServerBuilder;

public class GrpcServerSetup {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Server server = ServerBuilder.forPort(9090)
                .addService(new TradingServiceImpl())
                .intercept(new LoggingInterceptor())      // runs first
                .intercept(new RateLimiterInterceptor())   // runs second
                .build()
                .start();

        System.out.println("National Bank of India gRPC server on port 9090");
        server.awaitTermination();
    }
}
```

### How do you define a bidirectional streaming RPC in a proto file?

Declare `stream` on both the request and response.

```protobuf
syntax = "proto3";

package com.bank;

service TradingService {
    // Bidirectional stream: client sends subscriptions,
    // server sends price updates
    rpc StreamPrices (stream PriceRequest) returns (stream PriceUpdate);
}

message PriceRequest {
    string symbol = 1;    // e.g., "RELIANCE"
    string action = 2;    // "SUBSCRIBE" or "UNSUBSCRIBE"
}

message PriceUpdate {
    string symbol = 1;
    string price = 2;     // BigDecimal as string
    int64 timestamp = 3;
}
```

### How do you implement the bidirectional streaming service?

Return a `StreamObserver` for inbound messages and push updates on the response observer.

```java
package com.bank.grpc;

import io.grpc.stub.StreamObserver;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class TradingServiceImpl
        extends TradingServiceGrpc.TradingServiceImplBase {

    private final ScheduledExecutorService ticker =
            Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(1);

    @Override
    public StreamObserver<PriceRequest> streamPrices(
            StreamObserver<PriceUpdate> responseObserver) {

        Set<String> subscriptions = ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet();

        // Ticker pushes prices for subscribed symbols
        ScheduledFuture<?> tickerTask = ticker.scheduleAtFixedRate(() -> {
            for (String symbol : subscriptions) {
                BigDecimal price = new BigDecimal("2450.00")
                        .add(BigDecimal.valueOf(Math.random() * 10));
                responseObserver.onNext(PriceUpdate.newBuilder()
                        .setSymbol(symbol)
                        .setPrice(price.toPlainString())
                        .setTimestamp(System.currentTimeMillis())
                        .build());
            }
        }, 0, 500, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);

        // Inbound observer handles subscribe/unsubscribe
        return new StreamObserver<>() {
            @Override
            public void onNext(PriceRequest request) {
                if ("SUBSCRIBE".equals(request.getAction())) {
                    subscriptions.add(request.getSymbol());
                    System.out.println("Subscribed: " + request.getSymbol());
                } else {
                    subscriptions.remove(request.getSymbol());
                    System.out.println("Unsubscribed: " + request.getSymbol());
                }
            }

            @Override
            public void onError(Throwable t) {
                tickerTask.cancel(true); // stop ticker on error
                System.out.println("Client error: " + t.getMessage());
            }

            @Override
            public void onCompleted() {
                tickerTask.cancel(true); // stop ticker on client done
                responseObserver.onCompleted();
                System.out.println("Client stream completed");
            }
        };
    }
}
```

### How do you handle backpressure in bidi streaming?

Check `isReady()` on the `ServerCallStreamObserver` before pushing so a slow client does not cause unbounded buffering.

```java
package com.bank.grpc;

import io.grpc.stub.ServerCallStreamObserver;
import io.grpc.stub.StreamObserver;

public class BackpressureAwareStream {
    public void pushPrices(StreamObserver<PriceUpdate> responseObserver) {
        ServerCallStreamObserver<PriceUpdate> serverObserver =
                (ServerCallStreamObserver<PriceUpdate>) responseObserver;

        serverObserver.setOnReadyHandler(() -> {
            while (serverObserver.isReady()) {
                // Only push when the client can receive
                PriceUpdate update = generatePrice();
                serverObserver.onNext(update);
            }
        });
    }

    private PriceUpdate generatePrice() {
        return PriceUpdate.newBuilder()
                .setSymbol("RELIANCE")
                .setPrice("2455.50")
                .setTimestamp(System.currentTimeMillis())
                .build();
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes with gRPC streaming?

Forgetting to cancel the background ticker when the client disconnects leaks threads and memory.

```java
// WRONG: ticker keeps running after client disconnects
@Override
public void onCompleted() {
    responseObserver.onCompleted();
    // ticker still running! Memory and thread leak.
}

// CORRECT: cancel the ticker
@Override
public void onCompleted() {
    tickerTask.cancel(true);  // stop the background work
    responseObserver.onCompleted();
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- For distributed rate limiting across a fleet, use a sliding window in Redis so the limit is shared.
- Interceptors execute in registration order: add logging before rate limiting so rejected calls are also logged.
- Use `Context` and `Metadata` to propagate trace IDs and authentication tokens through the interceptor chain.
- Monitor the number of active bidi streams as a scaling metric.

```java
// Propagate trace ID through metadata
Metadata.Key<String> TRACE_KEY = Metadata.Key.of(
        "x-trace-id", Metadata.ASCII_STRING_MARSHALLER);
String traceId = headers.get(TRACE_KEY);
System.out.println("[gRPC] trace=" + traceId + " method=" + method);
```
